    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The grid on top is the mental picture. The line below is what memory actually holds. Reading that line left to right gives 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6 — the first row, then the second."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "The formula"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "text",
      "code": "offset of grid[r][c]  =  r x COLUMNS + c"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Read it as two steps. `r x COLUMNS` skips over `r` complete rows to land at the start of row `r`. Adding `c` walks `c` cells further along that row."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "For `grid[1][2]` in a 2 x 3 grid: `1 x 3 + 2 = 5`. Slot 5 holds the value 6, which is indeed row 1, column 2."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Who uses it"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Row-major is by far the more common choice: C, C++, Java, Python (including NumPy by default), JavaScript, Rust and Go all use it. If you have never explicitly chosen a layout, you have been using row-major."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "What this means for your loops"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Since consecutive **columns** are adjacent in memory, the fast traversal varies the column index in the inner loop:"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "// fast: moves along memory\nfor (let r = 0; r < rows; r++) {\n  for (let c = 0; c < columns; c++) {\n    process(grid[r][c]);\n  }\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Each step of the inner loop moves to the very next address. This is the order that keeps the cache full, and it happens to be the order most people write by instinct — which is why grid code is usually accidentally correct about performance."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The lesson after next shows what happens when you write the other one."
    }
  ],
